course: dictionary
lesson: 1671
title: Words for narrow places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. Break a hard word into parts you know. Chokepoint is made of choke, which means to get squeezed or blocked up, and point, which means a place. Put them together and you have a place where things get squeezed and blocked.
explain: «Most goods now cross the sea in standard steel boxes on giant ships, squeezing through a few narrow passages called chokepoints, like the Strait of Malacca or the Suez Canal.» The clues are squeezing and narrow passages. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word. A narrow place fits, so chokepoint must mean a narrow place.
explain: The word can also be used in a wider way. In a school fire drill, a crowded door or staircase is a chokepoint, because people slow down and get stuck there. Any point in a system where things slow down or get stuck can be called a chokepoint.
fact: A chokepoint is a narrow place where movement is squeezed together and can easily be blocked.
fact: «Chokepoint» can also mean any point where things slow down or get stuck, such as a crowded door.
fact: «A few narrow sea passages, called chokepoints, carry a huge share of world trade.»
fact: Famous sea chokepoints include the Strait of Malacca, the Suez Canal, the Strait of Hormuz and the Panama Canal.
fact: Armies have defended chokepoints, such as the pass at Thermopylae. A blocked chokepoint slows world trade.
exercise: «A few narrow sea passages, called chokepoints, carry a huge share of world trade.» Which word tells you that chokepoints are narrow? | *narrow | world | huge | carry
exercise: «Most goods now cross the sea ... squeezing through a few narrow passages called chokepoints.» What is a chokepoint? | A standard steel box | A ship that carries goods | A wide open ocean with no land nearby | *A narrow place where movement is squeezed and can be blocked
exercise: Ella says a chokepoint is a big open field. Which of these is a chokepoint? | A sunny beach | A huge empty desert | A wide open field | *A crowded door in a school
test: When a giant ship got stuck in the Suez Canal, what happened to world trade? | It stopped the sun from shining | It became cheaper | It grew faster | *It slowed down
test: Which best fits the wider meaning of chokepoint? | *Any point in a system where things slow down or get stuck | A long straight road with no cars | A place where it is always sunny | A wide river mouth
exam: Which sentence uses chokepoint correctly? | *Traffic often slows down at a chokepoint where the road narrows to one lane | The chokepoint was a long, wide sky | A chokepoint is a type of bird | Chokepoints are always made of gold
